Run the Pty Host as a worker_thread instead of a forked process.

Android 12+ kills background child processes past a system-wide limit of 32, and
every fork this product makes spends one for the lifetime of the window. Threads
are not processes. Hosting the Pty Host as a worker_threads Worker takes it off
that budget without rewriting it: bootstrap-fork installs process.send over
parentPort on the child side, so the module inside still sees the IPC channel it
was written against.

Three parts.

bootstrap-fork gains the bridge, before anything that gates on process.send. In a
real fork isMainThread is true and none of it runs, so the same file serves both.
The parent cannot close a Worker's channel the way it closes a fork's, so
shutdown is asked for in-band with __vsc_disconnect and the module runs its
normal teardown.

workerAsChildProcess.ts is a new adaptor presenting the small part of the
ChildProcess surface the IPC actually touches: send, connected, kill, pid, the
message/error/exit events, and stdout/stderr for the Extension Host that follows.
Nothing else is faked, so a new dependency on ChildProcess fails here rather than
behaving oddly on a device. Two details are load-bearing: a Worker rejects
--max-old-space-size in execArgv and wants it as resourceLimits instead, which
otherwise throws ERR_WORKER_INVALID_EXEC_ARGV before the module loads; and an
intentional kill is reported as (0, <the signal asked for>) because callers read a
non-zero code without a signal as a crash. kill() takes that signal and honours
SIGKILL by terminating at once; it used to take none, so a caller escalating past
the graceful path silently got the graceful path.

The graceful path itself waits for the worker's exit event. It used to pause for a
fixed 200ms and terminate whatever the module was doing, which is shorter than the
Pty Host's own teardown: that host writes a terminal's remaining output on its way
out, and anything still buffered was cut off mid-write with nothing logged. The
wait is still bounded, by WORKER_EXIT_GRACE_MS, because a Worker keeps its host
process alive and a module that never exits would otherwise hold the server open.

ipc.cp.ts picks between them on an explicit useWorker option, set at the Pty Host
construction site in nodePtyHostStarter.ts. It used to compare serverName against
the literal 'Pty Host', which is a log label: upstream renaming it would have
turned the comparison quietly false, the host would have gone back to forking, and
every gate here would still have passed, including this patch's own fingerprint.
An option the call site has to set fails to apply if that call site moves.

Only the Pty Host moves in this change; the file watcher keeps forking, and the
Extension Host is a separate step because it also has to stop passing sockets over
its channel.

